# Hosting A Web Server From Home

This guide uses a dedicated Ubuntu server, Docker Compose, a domain name, and Caddy. It exposes only the web server to the internet. Your app stays on Docker's private network.

## Why Docker And Caddy

Docker is not faster than running the app directly on Ubuntu. I used it here because it keeps each app's Python, Node, database, and system dependencies separate from the server and makes moving or rebuilding the app predictable. It is a good default when one home server may run several unrelated apps.

Caddy is also not required. It fills the same reverse-proxy role as Nginx, but automatically obtains and renews HTTPS certificates when the domain reaches ports 80 and 443. With Nginx, use Certbot or another ACME client for those certificates.

Nginx plus Gunicorn is a very good, lean choice for a Flask, Django, or other WSGI Python app. It runs directly from a Python virtual environment and `systemd`, so there is less Docker configuration to learn. Gunicorn should bind only to `127.0.0.1` or a Unix socket, while Nginx is the only process exposed publicly.

For a FastAPI app such as KC, use Nginx plus Uvicorn instead of plain Gunicorn because FastAPI is ASGI. Uvicorn can run multiple workers itself. For a Flask app such as SAS, Nginx plus Gunicorn is appropriate.

Choose one path per app:

- Use Docker plus Caddy when you want repeatable deployments, automatic HTTPS, or several isolated apps on the same server.
- Use Nginx plus Gunicorn when the app is WSGI Python and you want the smallest direct-to-Ubuntu setup.
- Use Nginx plus Uvicorn when the app is FastAPI or another ASGI Python app and you do not want Docker.

## Before You Start

You need:

- A 64-bit Ubuntu 22.04 or 24.04 machine that stays on.
- A domain name you control, such as `example.com`.
- Router access so you can reserve an internal IP and forward ports.
- A separate device or mobile data connection to test the public site.

Do not use a laptop that contains personal files as the public server. Keep a current backup of the app data before exposing it.

## 1. Prepare The Server

Install Ubuntu Server, create a normal administrator account, then update the machine:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y ca-certificates curl ufw
```

Set a fixed LAN address in your router's DHCP reservation page. For example, reserve `192.168.1.50` for this server. Do not rely on a manually typed static IP that can conflict with another device.

Use SSH keys, then turn off password login before exposing SSH:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.ssh
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
nano ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
sudo nano /etc/ssh/sshd_config
```

Set these values in `sshd_config`:

```text
PermitRootLogin no
PasswordAuthentication no
```

Check the configuration and restart SSH. Keep the current SSH session open until you have confirmed that a second session can sign in with the key.

```bash
sudo sshd -t
sudo systemctl restart ssh
```

Enable the firewall. This allows SSH, HTTP, and HTTPS only:

```bash
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw status verbose
```

## 2. Configure The Router And DNS

In the router, forward only these TCP ports to the reserved server IP:

```text
WAN 80  -> 192.168.1.50:80
WAN 443 -> 192.168.1.50:443
```

Do not forward app, database, Docker, SSH, or admin ports to the internet. Use SSH from your LAN or a VPN for administration.

Find your public IP with:

```bash
curl https://api.ipify.org
```

At your DNS provider, create an `A` record for `example.com` pointing to that public IP. Add `www` too if you want it. If your public IP changes, use your router's dynamic-DNS feature or a DNS provider API updater.

If your router has no port-forwarding option or the site cannot be reached from mobile data after forwarding, ask your ISP whether you are behind carrier-grade NAT. Port forwarding cannot work through carrier-grade NAT; use a tunnel or move the app to a hosted server instead.

## 3. Install Docker

Use Docker's official Ubuntu installation instructions. Do not use Docker's convenience script for this production server.

```bash
sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
sudo curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc
sudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc
sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.sources >/dev/null <<EOF
Types: deb
URIs: https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu
Suites: $(. /etc/os-release && echo "${UBUNTU_CODENAME:-$VERSION_CODENAME}")
Components: stable
Architectures: $(dpkg --print-architecture)
Signed-By: /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc
EOF
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin
sudo systemctl enable --now docker
sudo docker run hello-world
```

Only add a trusted administrator to the `docker` group. That group has effectively root-level control of the server.

```bash
sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"
```

Sign out and back in before using Docker without `sudo`.

## 4. Create The Site Folder

```bash
sudo mkdir -p /srv/my-site
sudo chown "$USER":"$USER" /srv/my-site
cd /srv/my-site
mkdir app
```

Put your application code in `/srv/my-site/app`. Do not commit `.env` files, private keys, database dumps, or uploaded customer data to Git.

Create `/srv/my-site/.env` for production secrets and restrict it:

```bash
nano .env
chmod 600 .env
```

Example `.env` values:

```text
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://...
JWT_SECRET_KEY=replace-with-a-long-random-value
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=...
```

Generate a random secret when needed:

```bash
openssl rand -base64 48
```

## 5. Run The App Behind Caddy

Create `/srv/my-site/Caddyfile` and replace `example.com` with your domain:

```caddyfile
example.com {
    reverse_proxy app:8000
}
```

Create `/srv/my-site/compose.yml`. Replace `./app` and the startup command with the app's actual Dockerfile and command:

```yaml
services:
  app:
    build: ./app
    env_file: .env
    restart: unless-stopped
    expose:
      - "8000"

  caddy:
    image: caddy:2
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "80:80"
      - "443:443"
    volumes:
      - ./Caddyfile:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile:ro
      - caddy_data:/data
      - caddy_config:/config
    depends_on:
      - app

volumes:
  caddy_data:
  caddy_config:
```

Important: only Caddy has `ports`. The application uses `expose`, so it is reachable only by Caddy inside Docker. Docker-published ports can bypass normal UFW rules, which is why this distinction matters.

Start the site and watch its logs:

```bash
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose ps
docker compose logs -f caddy
```

When DNS and router forwarding work, Caddy requests the certificate, renews it later, and redirects HTTP to HTTPS.

## 6. Verify From Outside Your Home

Turn off Wi-Fi on a phone and open `https://example.com`. Confirm:

- The browser reports a valid certificate.
- The site loads over HTTPS.
- `http://example.com` redirects to HTTPS.
- No database, admin, app, or Docker port is public.

Do not rely only on testing from inside your home network. Some routers do not support testing their own public address from the LAN.

## 7. Ongoing Maintenance

Run these monthly:

```bash
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
docker compose pull
docker compose up -d
docker image prune -f
```

Back up database data and upload folders to a location outside the house. Test restoring a backup before you need it. Keep an eye on disk use, logs, router updates, and failed certificate renewal messages.

Useful commands:

```bash
docker compose ps
docker compose logs -f app
docker compose logs -f caddy
docker compose down
docker compose up -d --build
```

